Thanks for pointing out your inspiring work and we have well read the content. We will add it to our reference and discuss the paper accordingly -- note the deadline of ICLR is 2 Oct. while the arxiv version is available on 19 Oct right after our submission to ICLR. Also we think there are some differences worth mentioning here. 

\textbf{1) General idea.} 

You adopted the framework of Moco. The InfoNCE used there aims to distinguish the distribution of positive pair and random generalized data (in line 68 of https://github.com/TengdaHan/CoCLR/blob/main/model/pretrain.py). Intuitively, the NeurIPS20 paper mainly wants to expand the positive set, while the negative is strictly correct.

In our paper, we observe something interesting in SimCLR. When batch size increases from (128 to 256), (256 to 512) and (512 to 1024), the accuracy improvement will increase significantly. But when batch size increases form (2048 to 4096) and (4096 to 8192), the accuracy wont increase as much as before. So what happened to this? One of the reason is larger batch size can involve more information of negative samples. In our guess, this is because with larger batch size, too more positive pairs are regarded as negative pairs, which cause the performance stagnation. 

\textbf{2) Technical details.}

The NeurIPS20 paper only expands the positive pairs, in line with the practice in Moco. While our method moves the top-$K$ closest negative pairs to positive pairs.
